00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:20.880
Webarchive files are used to save whole websites:&nbsp;
HTML, CSS, JS and images - everything in a single&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:28.800
file. It's an XML-based format and it looks&nbsp;
like this. The base64 encoded content is here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:36.720
and here is the origin. Yes, you heard me right&nbsp;
- there is an origin specified. Back in 2013, if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:42.960
you could control the contents of this file, you&nbsp;
could just put an arbitrary origin, encode the XSS&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:49.280
payload and you would be able to execute the code&nbsp;
on any domain and access any domain's cookies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:58.400
Such an attack is called universal XSS. It's not&nbsp;
a bug in a particular website but in the browser&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:04.960
which makes it insanely powerful. The attacker&nbsp;
can steal data from every website that you use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:11.760
so the potential impact is huge. According&nbsp;
to Google, almost as valuable as an RCE.

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:19.840
So back in 2013, if you could force the victim&nbsp;
into downloading and opening such a file,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:27.040
you could perform this attack. But it's not how&nbsp;
it works in 2022 with more security mechanisms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:35.680
in Safari and macOS but let's try to bypass them.&nbsp;
The attack has to start with phishing the victim&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:42.000
into visiting our website. It should not be a&nbsp;
big problem but every single user interaction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:48.560
makes the attack less and less likely. The first&nbsp;
problem that we encounter from here is prompt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:54.240
about downloading a file. Because the first&nbsp;
time you download a file from any domain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

